NAME

ulibLoadFileCat - Read a file entry from the file catalogue


SYNOPSIS

UBYTE ulibLoadFileCat(struct CatCommand *LFC)
                               a0


DESCRIPTION

This function accesses the file catalogue and attempts to load a file
entry (and possibly its description) into memory.

The parameters passed by way of a pointer to a (struct CatCommand).  The
fields currently used are described below (ignore the other fields):

   <Name>
      File name of entry to load.

   <File1>
      A pointer to a (struct FileCat) into which the file entry will be placed.
      If this is NULL, then the entry, if found, is not stored anywhere.

   <DataPosition>
      The file offset in the appropriate FileData.x for this file's catalogue
      information.

   <QdxPosition>
      The quick index file offset in either FileData.qdx or NewFileData.qdx;
      the WasInNewFileDataQdx bitfield flag reports which one.

   <GlobalFileIndexHandle>
      If not NULL, this must be a pointer to a file handle returned by the
      AmigaDOS Open() function, on the quick index file
      "XFILECAT:FileData.qdx".

      The file seek pointer MUST be at the beginning of the file; use a
      Seek(Handle, 0L, OFFSET_BEGINNING) if necessary.  The open mode should
      preferably be MODE_OLDFILE for this function, but this is not important.

      The handle will be left open by this function, but the current seek
      position will be undefined.

   <GlobalFileIndexHandle>
      Similarly, this must be a pointer to a file handle returned by the
      AmigaDOS Open() function, on the quick index file
      "XFILECAT:NewFileData.qdx".

      The file seek pointer MUST be at the beginning of the file; use a
      Seek(Handle, 0L, OFFSET_BEGINNING) if necessary.  The open mode should
      preferably be MODE_OLDFILE for this function, but this is not important.

      The handle will be left open by this function, but the current seek
      position will be undefined.

   <CatHandle>
      If not NULL, this must be a pointer to a FileData.x file handle, where
      x is the number of the section that the file is known to be in.  If
      this variable is used, ensure that the file actually *is* in the
      specified section, otherwise the function will fail.

      The file seek pointer does NOT have to be anywhere in particular;
      Xenolink will Seek() to the correct position from the data in the
      quick index entries above.

      The file open mode should preferably be MODE_OLDFILE for this function,
      but this is not important.

      The handle will be left open by this function, but the current seek
      position will be undefined.

      Use of this variable is NOT recommended at present.

   <CatTextHandle>
      If not NULL, this must be a pointer to a FileDesc.x file handle, where
      x is the number of the section that the file is known to be in.  If
      this variable is used, ensure that the file actually *is* in the
      specified section, otherwise the function will fail.

      The file seek pointer does NOT have to be anywhere in particular;
      Xenolink will Seek() to the correct position from the data in the
      quick index entries above.

      The file open mode should preferably be MODE_OLDFILE for this function,
      but this is not important.

      The handle will be left open by this function, but the current seek
      position will be undefined.

      Use of this variable is NOT recommended at present.

   <Index>
      If not NULL, this must be a pointer to a (struct Index), storage space
      for a quick index entry.  Whether this was an entry taken from
      FileData.qdx or NewFileData.qdx is determined by the
      <WasInNewFileDataQdx> flag below.

      Note that the contents of the index are only valid if the file was
      located successfully.

   <Flags>
      The following elements are defined:

         <LoadDescription> - Load the file's variable length description also.

                             If the file entry is located successfully and
                             this bit is set, then its description will be read
                             in to the appropriate fields within the
                             (struct FileCat).

                             You are responsible for freeing this memory.
                             This is done with the ulibFreeFileData()
                             function.

         <RetainHandles>   - Retain any file handles this function opens, and
                             store them in <GlobalFileIndexHandle>, etc.
                             Note that some, none, or all pertinent file
                             handles may be retained when this option is set;
                             the handles which are not NULL are "retained"
                             filehandles.

         <WasInNewFileDataQdx> - Reports whether the file's quick index entry
                                 was found in NewFileData.qdx or FileData.qdx
                                 (if the file was found at all).


RETURNS

0 - Failure (not found, or other error)
1 - Success
